SET
  title = 'Robots, voice agents and ChatGPT Health: July 24 AI news',
  excerpt = 'FLUX 3 moves visual AI toward robot learning. ChatGPT and Claude expand voice controls, and ChatGPT Health reaches more U.S. users.',
  content = '<p><strong>Four AI updates landed this week, and they have little in common on the surface.</strong></p><p>Black Forest Labs released a model for image, video and audio. OpenAI and Anthropic added more ways to direct AI agents by voice. OpenAI also opened ChatGPT Health to more people in the United States. Put together, they show where the companies are putting their effort: software that can take in more context and do more of the work.</p><h2>FLUX 3 is built for more than image generation</h2><p>Black Forest Labs released FLUX 3, a model family that works with image, video and audio. The company is developing an action model with mimic robotics for robot-learning research, and it has put FLUX 3 Video into early access. This does not mean AI robots are about to show up in every workplace. It does give researchers a model that can handle more visual information than a text prompt and a still image.</p><p><a href="https://bfl.ai/blog/flux-3" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the Black Forest Labs announcement</a>.</p><h2>Voice control is getting more useful</h2><p>ChatGPT Voice can now work with Work and Codex in the desktop app. Eligible Mac and Windows users can start a task, ask what is happening, interrupt it, or give a new instruction without typing each message. The feature still uses the tools and permissions available to that account or workspace.</p><p>Anthropic is moving in a similar direction. Superhuman reported that Claude Voice now supports Opus and Sonnet, and Anthropic''s July release notes describe new email and calendar actions for approved connected work setups. Voice is starting to look less like a chat feature and more like a practical way to manage an agent while you work.</p><p><a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001275-chatgpt-work-and-codex" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read OpenAI''s Work and Codex guide</a> · <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12138966-release-notes" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Anthropic''s release notes</a>.</p><h2>ChatGPT Health reaches more U.S. users</h2><p>OpenAI is rolling out Health in ChatGPT to eligible logged-in U.S. users aged 18 and older on Free, Go, Plus and Pro plans. People can choose to connect supported medical records and Apple Health, then use that information to review results, prepare for appointments, or look at changes in activity and sleep. It is available on web and iOS, and the rollout is gradual.</p><p>OpenAI says Health is meant to support medical conversations, not replace a clinician. That distinction matters. The product handles sensitive information, so users should be deliberate about what they connect and how they use the responses.</p><p><a href="https://openai.com/index/health-in-chatgpt/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read OpenAI''s Health in ChatGPT announcement</a>.</p><h2>What connects these updates</h2><p>Each company is trying to give AI more useful input. FLUX 3 takes in richer visual material. Voice agents accept spoken instructions while a task is in progress. ChatGPT Health can use information a person has chosen to connect. More context can make these tools more useful, but it also raises the stakes for permissions, privacy and human review.</p><h2>Editorial note</h2><p>The July 24 Superhuman newsletter surfaced these updates. Free Prompt Base checked the product details against the linked company sources before publishing this report. The cover is an original illustration, not a product screenshot.</p>',
  read_time = '3 min read',
  meta_title = 'Robots, voice agents and ChatGPT Health: July 24 AI news',
  meta_description = 'FLUX 3 moves visual AI toward robot learning. ChatGPT and Claude expand voice controls, and ChatGPT Health reaches more U.S. users.',
  updated_at = '2026-07-25 00:00:00'
